  "text": "Allophone: A distinct phonetic variant of a phoneme that occurs in a specific phonetic environment.\n\nAnaphora: A word or phrase that refers back to an earlier word or phrase in the discourse.\n\nApocope: The omission of a sound or syllable from the end of a word.\n\nArticulatory phonetics: The study of the physical mechanisms involved in producing speech sounds.\n\nAspirated consonant: A consonant produced with a strong burst of air from the lungs.\n\nAssimilation: A process in which a sound becomes more like a neighboring sound.\n\nBilabial consonant: A consonant produced with both lips.\n\nBidirectional language: A language that is written and spoken in two opposite directions.\n\nBilingualism: The ability to speak and understand two or more languages.\n\nBound morpheme: A morpheme that cannot stand alone as a word.\n\nBraille: A tactile writing system used by people who are blind.\n\nCalque: A loanword or phrase that is translated literally from another language.\n\nCleft sentence: A sentence structure that emphasizes a particular part of the sentence.\n\nComplement: A word or phrase that completes the meaning of a verb.\n\nConsonant: A speech sound produced with some degree of obstruction in the vocal tract.\n\nConsonant cluster: A group of two or more consonants occurring together in a word.\n\nConsonant sound: A sound produced with some obstruction in the vocal tract.\n\nConsonant vowel: A term not commonly used; vowels are typically considered to be the opposite of consonants.\n\nConsonantization: The process by which a vowel becomes a consonant.\n\nConsonantal: Relating to consonants or consonant sounds.\n\nConsonantless vowel: A vowel that occurs without a following consonant.\n\nConsonantalization: The process by which a vowel becomes a consonant.\n\nConsonantization: The process by which a vowel becomes a consonant.\n\nContrastive analysis: A method used to compare languages to identify differences.\n\nContour: The pitch pattern of a tone.\n\nConvergence: The process of adjusting speech to match the speech patterns of others.\n\nConventionalism: The idea that meaning is based on social convention.\n\nCorpus: A large collection of texts used for linguistic research.\n\nCorrespondence: A relationship between sounds in different languages.\n\nCreole: A language that develops from a pidgin and is used as a native language.\n\nCyclic stress: A stress pattern in which stress is distributed in a repeating pattern.\n\nDiphthong: A vowel sound that begins with one vowel quality and moves to another.\n\nDiscreteness: The property of a language's sounds being distinct and separable.\n\nDiphthongization: The process by which a vowel becomes a diphthong.\n\nDissimilation: A process in which a sound becomes less like a neighboring sound.\n\nDiphthongal vowel: A vowel that is a diphthong.\n\nDiphthongization: The process by which a vowel becomes a diphthong.\n\nDiphthong: A vowel sound that begins with one vowel quality and moves to another.\n\nDiphthongal: Relating to diphthongs.\n\nDiphthong: A vowel sound that begins with one vowel quality and moves to another.\n\nDiphthong: A vowel sound that begins with one vowel quality and moves to another.",
